  "average_rating": 4.3,
  "summary": "Bella Italia at Leicester Square (specifically the Cranbourn Street location, which serves as the primary hub for this postcode) is heavily penalized as a generic, commercialized tourist dining venue. It represents the literal antithesis of an insider culinary community hub. Operating as a highly corporatized high-street chain, it is designed strictly for transient tourists, theatre-goers, and families looking for safe, westernized Italian staples. It scores critically low across all culinary anthropology pillars due to its compromised menu, lack of geographic regionality, and total absence of local Italian diaspora patronage. While it functions physically as a full-service, sit-down establishment, it offers zero authentic or uncompromising gastronomic value.",
  "match_score": 12,
  "value_and_volume": {
    "score": 25,
    "analysis": "Portion sizes are strictly standardized, factory-controlled, and prepared for quick-service assembly rather than rustic, generous Italian hosting. Prices are heavily inflated due to prime West End real estate, meaning the value-to-cost ratio for culinary travelers is highly unfavorable."
  },
  "demographic_and_community_signal": {
    "score": 5,
    "analysis": "The dining room is exclusively filled with international tourists, retail shoppers, and transient families. There is absolutely no presence of the local Italian diaspora, neighborhood regulars, or culinary enthusiasts who would signal a living community hub."
  },
  "linguistic_and_insider_signal": {
    "score": 10,
    "analysis": "The entire menu and point-of-sale materials are in English with simplified descriptions. There are no untranslated regional specials or handwritten chalkboard menus in Italian. English is the mandated, functional language of mass corporate service."
  },
  "geographic_precision": {
    "score": 10,
